## What Ruby changed between step 00 and step 01

Confirmed by diffing `ruby/00_config` against `ruby/01_struct_skeleton`:

| Change | Ruby |
|---|---|
| **New** `lib/boukensha/tool.rb` | `Tool = Struct.new(:name, :description, :parameters, :block)` + `to_s` |
| **New** `lib/boukensha/message.rb` | `Message = Struct.new(:role, :content, :tool_use_id)` + `to_s` |
| **New** `lib/boukensha/context.rb` | Real `class Context` — holds `task`, `system`, `messages`, `tools`; methods `register_tool`, `add_message`, `tool_count`, `turn_count`, `to_s` |
| `lib/boukensha.rb` | Adds three `require_relative` lines for the new files |
| `lib/boukensha/config.rb` | **Removes** the `PROMPTS_DIR` constant |
| `prompts/system.md` | **Deleted** — step 01 ships no packaged default prompt |
| `examples/example.rb` | Rewritten: builds a `Context`, registers one `Tool`, adds two `Message`s, prints them |

`tasks/base.rb`, `tasks/player.rb`, `Gemfile` are byte-identical between the two
Ruby steps — the matching Python files in the copied folder are already correct
and need no touching.

> The last three rows are coupled: because `player`'s `prompt_override.system`
> is `true` and `.boukensha/prompts/player/system.md` exists, the example's
> `system_prompt` resolves via the **user override** and never needs a packaged
> default — which is why Ruby could delete both `PROMPTS_DIR` and
> `prompts/system.md` this step. The Python port mirrors that (see OQ 3).

