## The work — a checklist over the existing folder

### ➕ Add — three new modules under `boukensha/`

**`boukensha/tool.py`** ← `lib/boukensha/tool.rb`. Ruby
`Struct.new(:name, :description, :parameters, :block)`, positional construction,
custom `to_s`. Port as a `@dataclass` (the faithful Struct analogue — OQ 1):

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `Struct.new(:name, :description, :parameters, :block)` | `@dataclass` fields `name: str`, `description: str`, `parameters: dict`, `block: Callable` |
| `description.to_s[0..40]` | `str(self.description)[:41]` — **`0..40` is inclusive = 41 chars** → `[:41]`, not `[:40]` |
| `params=#{parameters.keys}` | Render as `[:direction]`, not `['direction']` (OQ 2): `"[" + ", ".join(f":{k}" for k in self.parameters) + "]"` |

Target `__str__`: `#<Tool name={name} description={desc[:41]} params=[:direction]>`.

**`boukensha/message.py`** ← `lib/boukensha/message.rb`. `@dataclass` fields
`role`, `content`, `tool_use_id=None` (last optional — the example omits it):

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `role` is a symbol `:user` | Python passes the string `"user"`; both render as `user` — no change needed |
| `id_tag = tool_use_id ? " [#{tool_use_id}]" : ""` | `f" [{self.tool_use_id}]" if self.tool_use_id else ""` |
| `content.to_s[0..60]` | `str(self.content)[:61]` — inclusive → `[:61]` |
| trailing `...` | Always appended, even when content is shorter than 61 chars (verified) — not conditional |

Target `__str__`: `#<Message role={role}{id_tag} content={content[:61]}...>`.

**`boukensha/context.py`** ← `lib/boukensha/context.rb`. A stateful plain class
(Ruby made it a class, not a Struct — keep that):

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `def initialize(task:, system: nil)` | `def __init__(self, *, task, system=None)` — keyword-only, `task` required |
| `@messages = []`, `@tools = {}` | `self.messages = []`, `self.tools = {}` |
| `register_tool(tool)` → `@tools[tool.name] = tool` | same, `self.tools[tool.name] = tool` |
| `add_message(role, content, tool_use_id: nil)` | `self.messages.append(Message(role, content, tool_use_id))` |
| `tool_count = @tools.size` / `turn_count = @messages.size` | `def tool_count(self): return len(self.tools)` / `turn_count` likewise |
| `task&.task_name` | `self.task.task_name() if self.task else None` — **note the `()`**: Python needs it to call the classmethod |

Target `__str__`: `#<Context task={task_name} turns={turn_count} tools={tool_count}>`.

